--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/city-kit.md
@@ -0,0 +1,307 @@
+# City kit
+
+The city kit is one set of highway, street and water metatiles. It sits at the same tile slots and the same metatile IDs in all three private primaries:
+
+- `taichung_xitun_private` (Xitun)
+- `taichung_greenway_private` (Greenway, Nantun North, Wanhe)
+- `taichung_old_core` (Old City)
+
+Every map can therefore paint it, and every map draws its neighbours' edge strips the same way, so seams always match. This is phase 1 of the city rework plan (the maps session's `docs/city-rework-plan.md`). It changes tilesets only: no map, secondary, palette or `src` file changes.
+
+The kit was built from native `6366b9e`.
+
+```sh
+python3 tools/taichung/kit/audit_primaries.py            # budget summary (--json for detail)
+python3 tools/taichung/kit/install_city_kit.py           # rebuild the nine primary files from 6366b9e
+python3 tools/taichung/kit/install_city_kit.py --check   # confirm they match the art
+python3 tools/taichung/kit/check_city_kit.py --self-test # the guard, with 12 corruption probes
+python3 tools/taichung/kit/city_kit_art.py --preview DIR # atlas and demo PNGs
+```
+
+## Budget
+
+`audit_primaries.py` counts a tile as used when any of these need it:
+
+- a primary definition that an exterior uses, in map.bin, border.bin or a `setmetatile` script;
+- any secondary definition;
+- a General animation slot;
+- a General metatile named in C, or a Cut swap reachable from used grass.
+
+| Primary | Tiles used | Free |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| XitunPrivate | 581 of 640 | 59 |
+| GreenwayPrivate | 571 of 640 | 69 |
+| OldCorePrimary | 563 of 640 | 77 |
+
+- **Tiles:** 40 are free in all three primaries. Four are held back for the city-v3 object library (`0x24E`, `0x24F`, `0x25E`, `0x25F`), which leaves 36. The kit uses 35 of them, so 1 stays spare (`0x125`). The retaining wall, kerbs and railings took six (`0x11A`–`0x11E`, `0x124`).
+- **Definitions:** 578 are free in all three. The kit uses 239 IDs in `0x063`–`0x157`. `0x076`–`0x07B` are retired: they held the old merged corner and are left as the base primary has them, so no later ID moved. The next free block runs `0x158`–`0x15A`, and `0x15B` is held back for code.
+- **Fit:** the kit fits the common primary set, so no secondary fallback is needed.
+
+Several choices keep the kit small:
+
+- **Streets reuse what exists.** The sidewalk is the existing paving metatile `0x175`, which Xitun already uses for its sidewalks. Its tiles (`0x152`, `0x153`, `0x162`, `0x163`) are identical in all three primaries.
+- **Water reuses General's animation.** Every water piece draws on General's own animated water fields (listed below). The kit adds only bank, rail and abutment overlays on the top layer, and no animation frame is redrawn. Editing those frames would change every pond in Kanto.
+- **Highways reuse tiles.** Each highway half is the other half turned 180°. The east–west highway is the north–south one transposed.
+- **Cars are recoloured, not redrawn.** Cars are a single drawing on the top layer: red in palette 2, blue in palette 3 and maroon in palette 1.
+- **One tile does two jobs.** A uniform tile is the highway lane in palette 5 and the under-deck shadow in palette 1.
+
+## Palettes and tiles
+
+Only existing primary palettes are used, and none is edited.
+
+| Palette | Used for |
+| --- | --- |
+| 1 | road bridge parapet, path bridge wood, maroon cars, under-deck shadow |
+| 2 | red cars |
+| 3 | water banks, abutments, blue cars |
+| 4 | General's animated water |
+| 5 | highway and streets |
+
+General's water fields, which the kit does not redraw:
+
+| Field | Tiles | Flow |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| still | `0x1A0`–`0x1A3` | pond ripple |
+| north | `0x1B2`–`0x1B5` | north, 2 px per frame |
+| south | `0x1B6`–`0x1B9` | south |
+| west | `0x1BA`–`0x1BD` | west |
+| east | `0x1BE`–`0x1C1` | east |
+
+The 35 kit tile slots are listed in `data/taichung/city-kit/kit.json` under `tileSlots`.
+
+## Passability
+
+Every kit metatile has behaviour 0. Water is never surfable and never a current, so nothing can push the player. Collision comes from the map word, as with every other metatile:
+
+- paint water and every highway cell with collision 1;
+- paint streets, crosswalks, sidewalks and bridge decks walkable.
+
+The guard rejects any surfable or current behaviour on kit water. Water overlays and cars are COVERED, which puts both layers below the player and objects.
+
+## How to compose
+
+### Highway (6 lanes)
+
+`hw-ns-0` … `hw-ns-5` are lanes from west to east. `hw-ew-0` … `hw-ew-5` are lanes from north to south.
+
+- **Layout:** each cell is one 16 px lane.
+  - Lanes 0 and 5 carry the guard rail on their outer edge.
+  - White dashes mark the lane boundaries 0|1, 1|2, 3|4 and 4|5.
+  - The double yellow median sits on 2|3.
+- **Cars:** `hw-ns-N-car-{red,blue,maroon}` and `hw-ew-N-car-*` put a car in lane N, and the cars keep right:
+  - north–south lanes 0–2 drive south and lanes 3–5 drive north;
+  - east–west lanes 0–2 drive west and lanes 3–5 drive east.
+
+  Sprinkle cars, roughly one in seven cells per lane.
+- **Corner (overpass):** where the two highways meet, the east–west highway (Hwy 74) crosses over the north–south one (Freeway 1), which carries on north under the deck.
+  - The deck is the straight east–west highway, `hw-ew-0`…`hw-ew-5`, right up to the map's west edge; cars may sit on it.
+  - Over the north–south highway's six columns, the deck's south lane is `hw-ew-5-parapet`: the kerb with parapet posts.
+  - The north–south highway's first row under the deck is `hw-ns-0-under-n`…`hw-ns-5-under-n`: the lanes with the deck's shadow on their top half, solid and then dithered.
+  - For the world's north-west corner (x −5…0, y −5…0): rows −5…−1 are `hw-ew-0`…`hw-ew-4`, row 0 is `hw-ew-5-parapet`, and row 1 of columns −5…0 is `hw-ns-N-under-n`.
+- **Under the deck (no longer drawn):** the board of 9 Nov raised the highway behind a retaining wall, so no street reaches it. These pieces stay in the kit:
+  - In the first column east of the north–south highway, use `st-ew-sidewalk-under-w`, `st-ew-asphalt-under-w` and `st-ew-centre-under-w`.
+  - In the first row south of the east–west highway, use `st-ns-sidewalk-under-n`, `st-ns-asphalt-under-n` and `st-ns-centre-under-n`.
+- **Highway bridges** (the SW and NE corners of the plan): the highway cells stay as they are. The river's first cell beyond the deck (beyond the wall, under the west highway) takes the shadow and abutments. The river's flow kind decides which pieces apply:
+  - an east- or west-flowing river passing under the north–south highway uses `wa-east-body-under-w`, `wa-east-bank-n-abut-w` and `wa-east-bank-s-abut-w` (or the same with `west`);
+  - a north- or south-flowing river passing under the east–west highway uses `wa-south-body-under-n`, `wa-south-bank-w-abut-n` and `wa-south-bank-e-abut-n` (or the same with `north`).
+
+### Retaining wall (2 wide)
+
+The highway's city side is a raised wall, `W` on the plan: rows 1–2 under the north highway (x 1…119) and columns 1–2 beside the west one (y 3…167). Paint every wall cell with collision 1.
+
+- **North wall:** row 1 is `wall-n-upper` (the coping, then a block course) and row 2 is `wall-n-lower` (a block course, then the dark foot).
+- **West wall:** column 1 is `wall-w-top` (the wall's top, in blocks) and column 2 is `wall-w-bank` (its shadow on a planted bank).
+- **Inner corner** (x 1–2, y 1–2): x 1 is `wall-w-top` in both rows, so the west wall's top runs up to the highway, and x 2 is the north face (`wall-n-upper`, `wall-n-lower`).
+- **Culvert:** where a two-wide channel flowing south leaves the north wall (Green River at x 96–97), row 1 is `wall-culvert-nw`, `wall-culvert-ne` and row 2 `wall-culvert-sw`, `wall-culvert-se`; the channel's own pieces (`wa-south-bank-w`, `wa-south-bank-e`) start at row 3.
+- **End at the east river:** the face runs to x 119 unchanged. The river beyond takes its usual under-deck bank, `wa-south-bank-w-abut-n` in row 1.
+- **Bridge side over the south river** (y 168 and below): x 1 carries on as `wall-w-top`; x 2 is `wa-<flow>-body-under-w`, with `wa-<flow>-bank-n-abut-w` and `wa-<flow>-bank-s-abut-w` on the bank rows.
+
+Only the wall's foot, its shadow and the arch are new tiles. The face and top reuse the abutment block (palette 3) under the road bridge parapet's coping (palette 1); the planted bank is General's leaf tiles `0x044`, `0x045`, `0x054` and `0x055` (palette 0), and the culvert's opening is the under-deck shadow over General's south-flowing water.
+
+### Streets (Xitun's profile, 5 wide)
+
+A street is sidewalk, three asphalt cells and sidewalk. The sidewalk is the existing paving metatile `0x175`.
+
+- **North–south street:** `0x175`, `st-asphalt`, `st-ns-centre`, `st-asphalt`, `0x175`.
+- **East–west street:** the same from north to south, with `st-ew-centre` in the middle.
+- **Crossings:**
+  - The 3×3 core where both streets' asphalt overlaps is `st-asphalt`.
+  - Crosswalks go where one street's sidewalk line crosses the other street's asphalt. Use `st-ew-crosswalk` on an east–west street's asphalt cells in the north–south street's sidewalk columns, and `st-ns-crosswalk` on a north–south street's asphalt cells in the east–west street's sidewalk rows.
+  - The four cells where the two sidewalk lines meet stay `0x175`.
+  - In a T junction, the closed side's sidewalk runs straight past, and no crosswalk crosses it.
+- **Street end (kerb):** a street whose end meets no other street stops one cell short. Its sidewalk wraps across the end, and the cells across the asphalt get bollards on the side the asphalt comes from: `st-bollards-n` ends a street that runs in from the north, and `st-bollards-s/w/e` likewise. The sidewalk columns stay `0x175`. Paint kerbs walkable.
+- **Railing:** every sidewalk cell that borders water (not a bridge) takes `st-rail-n/s/w/e` (the side the water is on), or `st-rail-nw/ne/sw/se` where water lies on two sides. Where water touches only its corner (a canal ending at a crossing sidewalk), the cell takes `st-rail-inner-nw/ne/sw/se`, named by that diagonal; it joins the side rail of the walk beside the canal to the run across the canal's end. Use it only where kit rails meet at that corner. Where the canal runs under a road bridge instead, the walk's rail stops at the end of the `br-ew-sidewalk-n/s` (or `br-ns-sidewalk-w/e`) parapet, and the corner cell stays `0x175`. The two already meet within 1 px, and an inner piece would leave rail stubs beyond the parapet. A 1-wide walk between water and a lot or park needs nothing else: the rail stands on the water edge and the rest of the cell is plain paving. A park path or plaza on secondary pavers draws the same two primary tiles, `st-rail-h` and `st-rail-v` (palette 3, upper layer), in its own secondary metatile at no tile cost: the run on a north or south edge, the side on a west or east edge, flipped horizontally for east.
+- **Parks** keep their own pale pavers from the secondary banks. Urban streets are this profile everywhere.
+
+### Water
+
+Each flow kind has one set of pieces: `wa-still-*` for lakes and ponds, and `wa-north/south/west/east-*` for rivers and canals. Pick the kind by the direction the water flows.
+
+- **Bodies two or more cells wide** (the plan's canals are 2 wide): `body`, `bank-n/s/w/e` (the bank is on that side), `outer-nw/ne/sw/se` (convex corners) and `inner-nw/ne/sw/se` (concave corners, named by the diagonal where land is).
+  - A 2-wide canal is a `bank-w` + `bank-e` pair, or a `bank-n` + `bank-s` pair.
+  - Where it turns, the outside cell is `outer-*` and the inside cell is `inner-*`.
+  - Where it opens into a river or lake, the two cells in the river's bank row become `inner-*`. In the demo, a south-flowing canal entering an east-flowing river's north bank uses `wa-east-inner-nw` and `wa-east-inner-ne`.
+  - Lakes and ponds use the same set from `wa-still-*`.
+- **One-cell canals:**
+  - `canal-ns` and `canal-ew` run straight.
+  - The corners `canal-ne`, `canal-nw`, `canal-se` and `canal-sw` are named by the two sides they open to.
+  - The T pieces `canal-t-n/s/w/e` are named by their closed side, and `canal-x` is the crossing.
+  - `mouth-n/s/w/e` goes in a wider river's bank row, named by the side the canal enters from.
+- **Road bridge:** a street crossing water keeps its asphalt cells. Its sidewalk cells become paving with a parapet on the outer half: `br-ns-sidewalk-w/e` or `br-ew-sidewalk-n/s`.
+- **Path bridge:** park paths cross on `pb-ns` / `pb-ew` planks, with rails `pb-ns-w/e` / `pb-ew-n/s`. A path crossing in a single line uses `pb-ns-single` or `pb-ew-single` in every cell.
+
+## The old 3-wide edge kit
+
+The 3-wide highway and river edge kit stays in the secondaries, at reserved slots `0x3E0` and up. The current maps still paint it, and they keep it until phase 2b redraws them from this kit. Retiring it is a phase 2b decision.
+
+## Adding pieces
+
+1. **Append, never reorder.** IDs follow the order of `city_kit_art.metatiles()` starting at `0x063`, so add new pieces at the end; the next free ID is `0x158`. Pieces that cost no tiles include `-under-e` and `-under-s` overpass variants, and cars in more palettes.
+2. **Tiles:** a new tile takes a slot from `spareTileSlots` in `kit.json` (one is left, `0x125`). Beyond those, re-run the audit to find more.
+3. **Rebuild and re-pin:** re-run `install_city_kit.py`, then put the new `kit.json` sha256 into `check_city_kit.py`'s `CONTRACT_SHA256`, and update its counts and slots.
+
+The other guards (`check_old_core`, `check_cmp`, `check_xitun_final`, `check_xitun_streets`) check underneath the kit through `check_old_core.city_kit_underneath()`. That function trusts the kit only while `kit.json` and the nine primary files match their pinned hashes. Re-pinning does not need any change in those guards.
