+# Landmark sources: set pieces 13–17
+
+Checked on 8 November 2026 against primary official sources. These are the Taichung Tourism and Travel Bureau, the Taiwan Tourism Administration, the National Museum of Natural Science, the Taichung City Cultural Heritage Office, the Taichung City Government and its bureaus and district offices, and the temple's own management office. All descriptions are short paraphrases. Hours and prices appear only where they show whether a place is open.
+
+Reading notes:
+- Coordinates come from each tourism entry's `place:location:latitude`/`longitude` HTML metadata, cross-checked against the map link on the page. They are representative points, not door positions. Latitude is given first.
+- The tourism bureau's `Dc.Description` metadata on every page holds the bureau's own office address in Fengyuan, not the landmark's address. Ignore it.
+- The zh-tw tourism pages build their body text with JavaScript, so they were read in a browser. The en pages serve their text as static HTML.
+- Distances and compass directions below are straight-line values computed from the official points, rounded. Neighbour points: Taichung Park `24.14431, 120.68571`, Old Taichung Station `24.13678, 120.68501`, Miyahara `24.13784, 120.68356` (all from `docs/landmark-sources.md`), and the National Museum of Natural Science `24.157233, 120.66606` (Taiwan Tourism Administration). The Green River uses the tourism bureau's Lyuchuan Canal point, `24.13900, 120.68451`. Its official English name is [Lyuchuan Canal / 綠川](https://travel.taichung.gov.tw/en/attractions/intro/1597), and that point sits on the stretch by the station and Miyahara. The river is a long channel, and its full course was not checked against an official map.
+
+## 13. Botanical Garden / 科博館植物園 (Tropical Rainforest Greenhouse / 熱帶雨林溫室)
+
+Verdict: verified. The plan note is right about the place. It is wrong about the shape.
+
+- [NMNS: Botanical Garden (en)](https://www.nmns.edu.tw/en/exhibitions/galleries/botanical-garden/index.html) and [植物園 (zh)](https://www.nmns.edu.tw/ch/exhibitions/galleries/botanical-garden/index.html): the 4.5-hectare garden of Taiwanese lowland ecology zones, with a tall rainforest greenhouse. The zh page calls the greenhouse a pagoda-like glass greenhouse with a Chinese flavour (倣塔造型), about 40 m tall overall. Garden and greenhouse together hold more than 800 plant species.
+- [NMNS: Tropical Rainforest Greenhouse (en)](https://www.nmns.edu.tw/en/exhibitions/galleries/botanical-garden/tropical-rain-forest-greenhouse/index.html): a giant glass greenhouse built on six sets of columns and seven circular beams, called the garden's most prominent landmark. It has themed rainforest sections and an Amazon fish aquarium in the basement.
+- [NMNS: 熱帶雨林溫室 (zh)](https://www.nmns.edu.tw/ch/exhibitions/galleries/botanical-garden/tropical-rain-forest-greenhouse/index.html): a suspended steel frame carrying a glass curtain wall. It stands 31 m above ground, goes 4.5 m below ground and is 56 m across. It has about 2,500 m² of display and about 500 species, plus an 8th-floor viewing deck. Simulated downpours and waterfalls run along the paths.
+- [NMNS: Museum history](https://www.nmns.edu.tw/en/about/museum-history/): the city gave 44,856 m² of land, and the Botanical Garden opened to the public on 23 July 1999. The page gives no separate date for the greenhouse.
