+## 16. Taichung Prefectural Hall / 臺中州廳
+
+Verdict: verified. Architect, year, mansard roof and arched windows are confirmed. The wall colour, central-pavilion and fountain parts of the plan note need correcting. It reopened on 15 October 2026.
+
+- [Taichung Tourism: Taichung Prefectural Hall (en)](https://travel.taichung.gov.tw/en/attractions/intro/1367): designed by Moriyama Matsunosuke in the Japanese era, the same architect as the Taipei and Tainan prefectural halls. It is French mansard in style, with a two-storey L-shaped plan facing east and a tower at the middle. The ground-floor entrance has Doric columns and the upper floor has Ionic columns, with recessed balconies for shade. The page carries a "Renovating" label (posted 2026-10-30) and still calls it a government building in use.
+- [Taichung Tourism: 臺中州廳 (zh)](https://taichung.travel/zh-tw/attractions/intro/56): a national monument mixing European Baroque and neoclassical styles. The main entrance is at the street corner, with wings spreading to either side. It is now an arts and exhibition venue run by the Cultural Affairs Bureau, open Tuesday to Sunday 09:00–17:00. One photo caption names its arched windows (拱型窗戶).
+- [Cultural Heritage Office: 臺中州廳](https://www.tchac.taichung.gov.tw/building?pid=18&uid=33): Moriyama Matsunosuke, a Government-General engineer, designed it after European Baroque models. The main block was begun in 1913 (Taishō 2) and its first phase finished the next year. Four further phases set its present size by 1934. The building sits on a street corner with the main entrance at the corner and wings to both sides. It was registered as a historic building in 2002, made a city monument in 2006 and a national monument on 25 April 2019. Main restoration ran 2019–2023 and reuse and landscape works 2025–2026. The Cultural Affairs Bureau moved in on 31 August 2026, and the Taichung Prefectural Hall Cultural Park (臺中州廳文化園區) opened on 15 October 2026. Address: 99 Minquan Road, West District.
+- [Taichung City Government news, 15 June 2026: 歷久彌新百年風華](https://www.taichung.gov.tw/8868/8872/9962/3297938): Cultural Affairs Bureau text. It says the building was built in 1913 and is related to the Presidential Office Building. Its main entrance at the corner was unusual for the time, with a corner tower as the focal point, a projecting carriage porch, symmetric flanking towers and long street wings. The facade uses stucco faux-stone, classical columns, a dome, triangular and rounded pediments and medallions. The roof is a double-pitched mansard with oeil-de-boeuf windows. The walls are warm red brick with grey-white faux-stone bands and a washed-pebble base. The windows have arched tops.
+- [Taichung City Government news, 15 October 2026: 台中州廳修復重啟](https://www.taichung.gov.tw/8868/8872/9962/3392631): the hall reopened as the Taichung Prefectural Hall Cultural Park after nearly ten years and over NT$600 million of work. The main building is a hollow square (口字型) around a central lawn. About 40% of the space is for arts, and more than 4,200 ping of outdoor green space is open. Captions mention the mansard roof, bull's-eye windows and dormers.
+- [Taichung City Government news, 2023: 百年古蹟台中州廳限定開放](https://www.taichung.gov.tw/2518459/post): the mansard roof has two pitches and carries dormers (老虎窗) and bull's-eye windows. The exterior imitates classical architecture mixed with early colonial veranda style.
+- Official tourism photo (cover image of the zh/en entries): a white-stucco corner block with a columned carriage porch under a low dome, and a white columned central bay with a curved gable. The long wings are red brick with white horizontal bands and arched windows. The mansard roof is dark grey with dormers, and palm trees stand along the front.
+- Coordinates: latitude `24.13856`, longitude `120.67848`, from the tourism metadata on both language entries, matching the en map link. The Cultural Heritage Office map marker is `24.1393410, 120.6789550`.
+- Plan note check:
+  - "Japanese-era civic hall": supported. Architect Moriyama Matsunosuke, begun 1913, expanded in phases to 1934. Style is described as mansard, Baroque and neoclassical.
+  - "White walls": wrong as stated. The Cultural Affairs Bureau describes red-brick walls with grey-white bands and a pebble-wash base, and the official photo agrees. White stucco covers mainly the corner entrance block, the porch and the columns. Draw red brick with white bands, and white stucco only on the entrance block.
+  - "Arched windows": supported.
+  - "Slate mansard roof": the mansard is supported (two pitches, dormers, bull's-eye windows). No source says slate. The photo shows dark grey, so a dark grey roof is safe but "slate" is unsupported.
+  - "Central pavilion": partly. The main entrance is at the street corner. It has a corner tower, a projecting carriage porch with a low dome and paired columns, and symmetric flanking towers. The en tourism page does describe a tower in the middle of the L-shaped front. A central pavilion reads correctly only if the corner faces the viewer. Give it a columned porch and a small dome.
+  - "Forecourt fountain": unsupported. No official source mentions a fountain. The sources describe a central lawn inside the hollow-square plan and open green space around the park. Leave out the fountain, or use lawn.
+- Position: about 0.52 km west of Miyahara and 0.6 km west of the Green River point. It is about 0.7 km west-northwest of Old Taichung Station and about 1 km southwest of Taichung Park. It is about 0.42 km south of the Second Market (the tourism nearby list gives 424 m) and about 2.4 km south-southeast of the Science Museum. The Liuchuan Canal waterfront point is about 0.47 km north-northwest.
+- Status: open since 15 October 2026 as the Taichung Prefectural Hall Cultural Park (Cultural Heritage Office, city news and the zh tourism entry). The en tourism entry's "Renovating" label is out of date.
